    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h4>Q: are you actually a professional cook at all</h4>
    <p>A: no, unless you count briefly working at the college dining hall making omelettes on a Mongolian grill</p>
    <h4>Q: what's the tech stack for this website</h4>
    <p>A: MEVN stack (MongoDB, Express, Vanilla JS for some reason, Node), plus <a href="https://ejs.co/">EJS templates</a> to make building the pages easier</p>
    <p>This technically isn't part of the tech stack but I also use <a href="https://dillinger.io/">Dillinger</a> to convert my recipes' markdown into html, which makes my life a lot easier</p>
    
    
    <h2>Boring stuff for nerds</h2>
    <h3>Changelog</h3>
    <h4>Version: 1.09 </h4>
    <ul>
        <li>A bunch of updates to improve mobile view (theoretically, nothing should change if you view the website on a computer)</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>Version: 1.08a (June 11 2026)</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>Fixed bug where a search with tags + empty query would sometimes break the page</li>
        <li>Fixed bug where a search with an empty query would sometimes show as "undefined"</li>
        <li>Added easter eggs to the search bar</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>Version: 1.08 (June 8 2026)</h4>
    <ul>
        <li>This is the search update! Search functionality has now been added through MongoDB Atlas's search index feature. Some notes on the feature:</li>
        <ul>
            <li>Search bar added to the nav bar!</li>
            <li>Search bar also added to the <a href="/recipe-index">all recipes</a> page.</li>
            <li>Swapped analyzer to lucene.english. The main advantage of this is that singular/plural words are treated as equivalent (i.e. searching for "lemon" or "lemons" will give you the same results)</li>
            <li>Tag filters are still functional and can be used in tandem with search</li>
            <li>Autocomplete is available on recipe titles (i.e. searching for "yogur" will fetch results for "yogurt" if that is in the title)</li>
            <li>Synonym mappings added for some key words (i.e. searching for "green onion" and "scallion" will yield the same results)</li>
            <li>Some input handling has been added for empty searches and searches containing only stop words (like "the" or "an")</li>
        </ul>
        <li>Other changes:</li>
        <ul>
            <li>Robots.txt and sitemap.xml files added for SEO purposes</li>
            <li>The navbar got some styling updates, mostly so it looks nicer on mobile</li>
            
        </ul>
    </ul>
